1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,881
A proof is a static claim.

2
00:00:01,881 --> 00:00:06,583
What comes out of the compiler is a
program with no prover in it.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:11,626
Compilation erases whatever exists only
for the proof.

4
00:00:11,626 --> 00:00:16,636
Proofs are propositions, and propositions
carry no runtime data.

5
00:00:16,636 --> 00:00:20,001
What is left is the algorithm and its
data.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:27,828
The guarantees are static, so nothing is
re-checked at runtime, and the cost of
having them is zero.

7
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:37,123
Lean elaborates to its own intermediate
representation, emits C, and links a small
runtime into a native binary.

8
00:00:37,123 --> 00:00:48,604
Memory is managed by reference counting
rather than a tracing collector, so
destruction is deterministic, and a
uniquely owned value can be updated in
place.

9
00:00:49,833 --> 00:01:03,574
In this tutorial's own measurement, the
extracted sort compiled to a small binary,
produced byte-identical output, and ran
about twenty-seven times faster than
interpreting the same file.

10
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:08,031
Zoom out, and the whole design is a
staircase.

11
00:01:08,031 --> 00:01:13,176
Within a level you optimise: rewrite the
program, keep its behaviour.

12
00:01:13,176 --> 00:01:20,261
Between levels you lower: a source
language becomes a simpler one, and
eventually machine code.

13
00:01:20,261 --> 00:01:23,766
Every vertical edge carries a refinement
proof.

14
00:01:23,766 --> 00:01:28,241
This stack is a proposed architecture, not
a settled result.

15
00:01:29,867 --> 00:01:34,281
Next, what a verified program still has to
assume about the world.

